Elevator System

(a)

Conventional multi-elevator building: users press UP/DOWN on a common hall panel, 
the elevator system selects the best elevator, that elevator arrives, and the user 
selects the destination using the elevator's internal panel.

Building has multiple floors.
Building has multiple elevators.
Every floor has a hall panel with : UP , DOWN
User presses UP/DOWN → system assigns an elevator.
Elevator arrives → doors open.
User enters → selects destination using internal panel.
Elevator moves between floors.
Elevator can stop at intermediate floors.
Elevator doors can open/close.
Elevator can receive new requests while already moving
Multiple users can request elevators simultaneously.

(b)
Identify the core entities

ElevatorSystem : List<Elevator> , ElevatorAllocationStrategy
Responsible for : managing elevators , receiving external requests , selecting an elevator
                  
Elevator : 
Responsible for : current floor , direction , state , pending destinations , door operations

class Elevator{
        int currentFloor;
        Direction direction;
        ElevatorState state;

        void move();
        void addDestination(int floor);
        void openDoor();  
        void closeDoor();
}

HallPanel
Responsible for capturing : Floor 5 , UP
It shouldn't decide which elevator to use.

InternalPanel
Responsible for: User → presses 8
It sends destination 8 to the assigned elevator.

ElevatorRequest
Represents a request such as: sourceFloor = 5 , direction = UP

Elevator state
enum ElevatorState { IDLE , MOVING , DOOR_OPEN , OUT_OF_SERVICE }
enum Direction { UP, DOWN, IDLE }

Elevator Allocation Strategy
This is probably the most important design decision.

interface ElevatorAllocationStrategy {
          Elevator selectElevator(
                 List<Elevator> elevators,
                 ElevatorRequest request
          );
}

LeastBusyElevatorStrategy , DirectionAwareStrategy , NearestElevatorStrategy

Scheduling Algorithm (how an elevator processes its destinations.)
-> SCAN Algorithm

External vs Internal Requests

External request

class Door {
    void open();
    void close();
}

Elevator ◆── Door
ElevatorSystem ◆── Elevator
ElevatorSystem ──→ ElevatorAllocationStrategy

Dependency Inversion Principle
ElevatorSystem depends on: ElevatorAllocationStrategy , rather than a concrete NearestElevatorStrategy

Concurrency

Imagine only one elevator is available.
Two users simultaneously request it.
Both could potentially see: Elevator A = available and both try to assign it.

The key concept is: CHECK → ASSIGN must not allow another thread to intervene incorrectly.

Multiple Requests
You need some form of Request Queue .

Failure/edge cases

Elevator unavailable -> OUT_OF_SERVICE. Don't assign new requests to it.
No elevator available -> Request waits in queue.
Elevator is full -> Depending on requirements, don't assign additional passengers.

 User presses UP -> HallPanel creates ElevatorRequest -> ElevatorSystem receives request ->
 ElevatorAllocationStrategy selects Elevator B -> Elevator B moves toward floor 5 ->
 Elevator B reaches floor 5 -> Door opens -> User enters -> User presses 8 on InternalPanel ->
 Elevator B adds destination 8 ->  Elevator moves toward 8 ->  It may serve compatible intermediate requests
 -> Reaches floor 8 -> Door opens

